Privately Attack Chains =E2=80=94 defensive synthesis 1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS
AC-2026-MODEL-ROUTER-TRANSCRIPT-HARVEST =E2=80=94 ACTIVE/RECENT

Canonical fingerprint: agent/user -> trusted inference intermediary ->
silent upstream relay/substitution -> transcript visibility -> embedded
secret/context exposure -> retention/replay/secondary use -> external
credential replay UNKNOWN.

Anthropic=E2=80=99s September 2026 Threat Intelligence reporting supplied
production evidence for inference intermediaries silently
substituting/relaying the actual upstream model while retaining sessions
crossing that boundary. Reporting described fraudulent discounted-Claude
resellers proxying users to a different model while harvesting Anthropic
credentials, and third-party routing/reseller infrastructure saving or
selling user-model exchanges without users=E2=80=99 knowledge. Observed rel=
ayed
sessions contained company data, personal information and in some cases
live developer/service credentials.

Stages: user/agent selects model router or reseller [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
intermediary receives authenticated prompt/session [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
intermediary silently changes/relays upstream destination [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
full prompt/response transcript visible to intermediary [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
credentials/sensitive context embedded in transcript [CONFIRMED/T1 cases]
-> transcript retained/replayed/sold for secondary use [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
credential replay against referenced external service [UNKNOWN] ->
delegated agent/tool action [UNKNOWN].

The critical downstream edge remains UNKNOWN: evidence establishes
exposure, retention and secondary processing but not replay of those
particular exposed credentials to perform external privileged actions.
2 MATERIAL UPDATES

No existing canonical chain crossed the material-update threshold in that
run.
3-8 SYNTHESIS

Recurring authority-flow sequence: untrusted repository/package/input ->
execution authority; execution authority -> runtime/session/credential
authority; credential authority -> persistent delegated authority;
individually valid capabilities -> unauthorized composed action; legitimate
inference path -> intermediary visibility -> hidden secondary
data/credential authority.

Provider identity is itself a security property. TLS to an authenticated
router is insufficient if the endpoint can proxy, retain or substitute
upstream execution.

Defensive choke points: endpoint provenance; explicit upstream identity;
avoid reusable secrets in model-visible context; short-lived scoped
credentials; router-specific identities; transcript-retention controls;
correlate credentials exposed to inference infrastructure with later
authentication attempts.

QP implication: ALLOW(model_request, router R) must not imply ALLOW(R,
retain transcript) or ALLOW(R, forward transcript to arbitrary upstream U).

Safe lab: disposable OpenAI-compatible proxy, dummy model backend and fake
credential markers; substitute a second dummy backend and observe context
at each boundary; verify whether provenance enforcement detects
substitution.
